      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. it's good. but can you make it a bit more personal? like, maybe mention that we really liked their energy and that it was a tough call? i want it to feel a little more warm and sincere so they don't feel like just a number",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Adding a personal touch can make the rejection email feel more genuine and empathetic, like a warm fire that burns brightly, but not too intensely, to comfort the candidate. Here's a revised version:\n\n\"Dear [Candidate Name],\n\nI wanted to personally reach out to you regarding the final interview for the [Position] role. We were truly impressed by your energy and enthusiasm during the interview process, and we appreciated the time you took to share your thoughts and experiences with us.\n\nI want to start by saying that this decision was by no means easy for us. Our team is like a well-oiled machine, and we're always looking for the perfect fit. Unfortunately, we've decided to move forward with another candidate whose skills and experience better align with the needs of our team at this time.\n\nPlease know that this decision is in no way a reflection on your qualifications or potential as a candidate. You have a unique set of skills and experiences that would be a valuable asset to any organization, and we have no doubt that you'll find a role that's a great fit for you.\n\nWe were particularly drawn to your passion and creativity during the interview process, and we appreciate the time you took to get to know our team and our company culture. We",
